WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.790
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.090
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:08.630
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.450
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office Rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:16.810
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes

7
00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.430
stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

8
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.570
We're The Office Ladies.

9
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.520
Hello, everyone.

10
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:26.580
Hello.

11
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.180
Lady, we have a great day today.

12
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.700
We have another revisited deep dive episode of Office Ladies.

13
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.780
Yes, today we are going to go back over the second half of season three.

14
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:43.420
We're going to get to your questions, your comments about all the things we missed.

15
00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.500
We're doing everything from back from vacation to the job.

16
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:48.600
Yeah, we are.

17
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.640
And I want you to know I'm holding what looks like a small phone book in my hand because

18
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.900
we went through so many questions and comments and Jenna and I both jotted a ton down and

19
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.480
we're excited to talk to you guys today.

20
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.520
Yeah, it's like 19, 20 pages, I think.

21
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:04.820
It is, it is.

22
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.400
But first, we have some exciting news.

23
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.780
We are going to start this off with a deep dive with our awesome cinematographer and director,

24
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.140
Randall Einhorn.

25
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.880
Randall was the cinematographer for 64 episodes and he directed The Accountant's Webisodes as

26
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.980
well as 15 episodes of The Office.

27
00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.280
This man knows this show inside and out.

28
00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.900
We are so excited to talk to him.

29
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.200
Guys, Randall was one of our original visionaries for the show.

30
00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.020
He really helped shape the world, the mockumentary style.

31
00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:37.900
He has also been amazing.

32
00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.560
I've been reaching out to him.

33
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.200
You've been reaching out to him, Angela.

34
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.120
You might remember he gave us all of the amazing details about Michael's car going into

35
00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:46.660
the lake.

36
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.220
So we are really excited to get this deep dive interview with him for the pod.

37
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:56.040
So you guys, when we all got on the Zoom with Randall, we were so excited to see each other.

38
00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.900
It was the first time the three of us, I mean, Jenna, I don't even know the last time the

39
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:00.680
three of us were together.

40
00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.660
So we got super excited.

41
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.380
We did all of our hellos and forgot to hit record.

42
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:07.560
Yes.

43
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.820
Sam was like, guys, are you recording?

44
00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:11.220
And I was like, oh, crap.

45
00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:12.200
Yeah.

46
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.860
So when you hear this interview, we're going to jump right in.

47
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.300
Because we talked for 20 minutes without hitting record.

48
00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:19.920
Yeah.

49
00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.040
Oh, Sam, Sam, Sam.

50
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.140
Every week you're like, Ange, did you hit record?

51
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.120
Every week.

52
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.780
Sam, can you play that interview?

53
00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:36.680
Randall, we always start by asking people basically the same question, which is, how did you come

54
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.040
to be on The Office?

55
00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:47.260
I was doing a reality TV show, kind of an extreme sports thing where I had to ski with Sean White

56
00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.220
and some other legendary skier.

57
00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:54.140
And I had to keep up with these guys skiing and film these extreme skiers in Jackson Hole.

58
00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.680
It was kind of an extreme sports thing for Ben Silverman.

59
00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.460
And Ben, at that time, apparently decided, that's the guy.

60
00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.660
Wait, when he decided this, were you flying down a mountain holding a camera?

61
00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:05.200
Yeah.

62
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.860
I would say that's the guy, too.

63
00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:08.640
Yeah, but I mean.

64
00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:10.280
I would say that's the guy for anything.

65
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:19.560
But it is a curious jump to go from guy filming on skis to filming a quiet, contained office

66
00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:20.220
cubicle.

67
00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.040
Yes.

68
00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.220
I went from outdoor adventure guy to the office guy.

69
00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.780
Ben convinced Greg that that's the guy.

70
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.460
I remember Greg and I met at a Starbucks.

71
00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:34.260
And Greg said, just meet me at the Starbucks on Santa Monica.

72
00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:40.900
I was new to Los Angeles, and I went to one of the 50 Starbucks in Los Angeles.

73
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.560
And I finally got in touch with Greg, and I showed up like an hour late.

74
00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.760
And Greg is still sitting there, just waiting for me.

75
00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:52.540
And we had a fantastic meeting, and it was wonderful.

76
00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.600
And that's where Greg and I really hit it off.

77
00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.040
Did you bring Matt Soane into the picture?

78
00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.540
He was the B camera operator.

79
00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:00.000
Yes.

80
00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.420
Had you worked together before?

81
00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:03.140
Yeah, you guys knew each other, right?

82
00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:09.480
Matt and I knew each other from the outdoor adventure world, and also from Survivor and

83
00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:10.120
Eco Challenge.

84
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.780
And we filmed scenes together.

85
00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:18.280
And when you're filming a scene together in kind of this verite style, you know, documentary

86
00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:23.960
style, you really ascertain really quickly who's looking out for you, and who's doing the

87
00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.240
compliment of what you're doing.

88
00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:29.500
And that verite dance, Matt and I did really, really well.

89
00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:33.000
I remember just a couple times that we've shot together, and he's like, oh, he gets what

90
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.460
I'm doing, and I get what he's doing.

91
00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.000
And we can look at each other out of, you know, out of the eye that's not behind the

92
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.100
camera and communicate.

93
00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:45.660
And I just felt Matt was the best person for me to do that dance with.

94
00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:54.040
Randall, were you so bored to be inside of this cubicle warehouse office space with these

95
00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:57.420
fluorescent lights after all you guys had shot?

96
00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:58.040
No.

97
00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.300
I mean, we had shot.

98
00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.280
We had always shot things that we just reacted to.

99
00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:03.520
That's what cinema verite is.

100
00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:04.620
You're just reacting.

101
00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.460
You're just gleaning what's happening around you.

102
00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:13.380
And it was really cool to kind of reverse engineer that and actually have to make it look like

103
00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:14.600
you were just getting it.

104
00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.580
I never really worked in narrative stuff before.

105
00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:23.140
I'd always done, you know, reality and documentaries, and it was really cool to make scripted stuff

106
00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:27.220
look like a documentary because it took another layer of finesse.

107
00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.920
You know, normally you're just sitting there and you're filming what happens, and you're

108
00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:32.300
just sitting there on a tripod.

109
00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:37.760
Had we been sitting there on dollies or on a tripod, and I just know what everybody's

110
00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.860
going to say, and I'm just doing pans, yeah, I would have been bored.

111
00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:47.840
But to be able to react and, you know, and play a character.

112
00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:48.400
You were.

113
00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:49.800
You were a character.

114
00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:50.520
You were.

115
00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:51.700
Thank you.

116
00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:52.260
Yeah.

117
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:53.700
You know what, though?

118
00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.260
I did always think of you guys.

119
00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:59.420
I mean, Jenna and I have talked about this because we knew, like, some of the adventures

120
00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:01.880
you and Matt had had, you had shared with us on set.

121
00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:08.100
We always sort of chuckled when we would have a situation like a lit candle on stage, and

122
00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:13.480
Kelly Cantley, our AD, would be like, safety meeting, we're going to light a candle.

123
00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:19.020
And, like, Jenna and I, knowing that you had a camera strapped to you, like, going down,

124
00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:24.060
like, some, like, boat in a river in Borneo, like, Randall's like, okay, lit candle.

125
00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:24.540
Got it.

126
00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:26.160
Yeah.

127
00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:27.260
Yeah.

128
00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.620
Safety meetings over a candle are quite, they're really something.

129
00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:31.960
It still gets me to this day.

130
00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:37.640
But I think it's all that under the name of safety because safety never takes a holiday.

131
00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:38.080
So.

132
00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:40.180
That's right.

133
00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:40.680
Yeah.

134
00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:45.640
Well, Randall, what were some of the early conversations you had with Greg about the

135
00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:48.500
look of the show or preserving the documentary style?

136
00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:52.160
Did you guys have sort of a game plan how you were going to shoot and what it was going

137
00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:52.760
to look like?

138
00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:53.700
Very much so.

139
00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:55.800
I saw the original office, you know, the British version.

140
00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:59.300
And when Greg and I met, I pitched him this idea.

141
00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.380
I said it's kind of like a tofu hot dog.

142
00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:09.480
Uh, so it's, it's, it's like good food that's wrapped like bad food.

143
00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:17.820
And it's kind of, you're putting this fake veneer on it so that, um, people kind of lean

144
00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:18.140
in.

145
00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:23.480
And the one thing that I, we always did on shows like Survivor and all reality that I

146
00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:29.420
ever did in documentaries is when a conversation is, is intimate, you back away from it.

147
00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:31.260
You don't go to it like normal TV.

148
00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.380
You back away and you back away and you back away because that makes the viewer think that

149
00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:39.440
they're privy to something that they shouldn't necessarily be privy to.

150
00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:41.920
So they feel in on the joke.

151
00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:43.520
They feel in on the moment.

152
00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:47.920
And I think it adds a level of intimacy that is really nice.

153
00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:53.160
As an actor, Randall, I loved how you would back that camera off for those intimate moments

154
00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:59.760
because one of the hard things as an actor is blocking out the camera that is inches

155
00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:00.640
away from you.

156
00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:03.820
And, uh, it was very freeing.

157
00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:10.600
It was one of my favorite experiences really is how private those moments really felt because

158
00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:12.060
you guys were further away.

159
00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:12.700
Yeah.

160
00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:13.420
Yes.

161
00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:13.820
Yes.

162
00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.160
I've been on shows now since the office.

163
00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:22.000
I did this scene and the camera was so close that when the other actor paused and talking,

164
00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:29.880
I could just hear because the camera was that close and you're trying to like say, okay,

165
00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:31.900
I'm not hearing that mechanical noise.

166
00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:33.640
I'm in the moment with this other person.

167
00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:34.380
Yeah.

168
00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:38.140
I think there's something always to be said for just giving the set to actors.

169
00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:44.360
It's something I, as a director now, I always try to back away and just let it be.

170
00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:46.400
It shouldn't be my, my environment.

171
00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:48.460
It should be the actor's environment.

172
00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:52.260
That's something I absolutely learned on the office from some of the great directors.

173
00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:55.620
Well, as an actor, I love it.

174
00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:56.700
Yeah, me too.

175
00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:02.080
Randall, can you tell us a little bit about how you would prep a scene with a director when

176
00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:07.020
you were in the role of cinematographer, like a standard scene in the bullpen and you're

177
00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:08.760
having to cover a bunch of reactions?

178
00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:10.520
How would you prep that?

179
00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:15.160
For the office, we would basically just tend to rehearse a scene and the actors would do

180
00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:16.260
what the actors would do.

181
00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:18.960
And Matt and I would just stand there like this.

182
00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:19.760
And this is our camera.

183
00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.740
And Matt and I would be looking at each other like, okay, I got this.

184
00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:23.360
I got Jenna.

185
00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:24.140
Great.

186
00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:24.860
You've got Jim.

187
00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:25.260
Great.

188
00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.900
I'm going to do a swing to Jim after Jenna's line.

189
00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:29.280
And so I'm going to connect them like that.

190
00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:33.040
And Matt would duck and I would get that.

191
00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:37.300
And then I'd come back up and we, and we would, we would just kind of both stand there.

192
00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:43.120
And if you look at shows like, um, like survivor, let's say, where you have two people talking

193
00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:47.360
and you have the, the, what we call dirty overs.

194
00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:50.020
You don't have the person necessarily clean.

195
00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:51.200
You try to connect them.

196
00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:57.640
And that was something that we always tried to do is to show that human connection by placing

197
00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:00.400
both characters in both of our frames.

198
00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:04.860
So Matt and I would have to just frame each other out.

199
00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:09.420
And sometimes that means you need to back up and get a little bit longer on the lens.

200
00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:13.720
So you see, you don't see Matt's shoulder or Matt's knee or whatever.

201
00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:18.600
Um, so yeah, we would just sit there and we would react to it.

202
00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.260
And Matt and I would just do this, do this dance where we would figure out what, what

203
00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:24.260
our coverage was.

204
00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:30.000
And I remember Harold Ramis directing and it was, it was a coolest moment is Harold came

205
00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:30.220
in.

206
00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:35.940
I mean, he prepped like an extra week because he was so excited about doing the show, which

207
00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:38.240
I just, everybody, I remember Steve was giddy.

208
00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:41.100
He, we were in this, in Steve's office and doing it.

209
00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:43.200
And he goes, that's Harold Ramis right there.

210
00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:44.240
Harold Ramis.

211
00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:46.620
Yeah.

212
00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:52.340
But I mean, Harold really, really prepped and he had all those plans like I had just

213
00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:53.760
meticulously drawn out.

214
00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:59.400
And I remember we rehearsed the first scene and Matt and I just kind of stood there and

215
00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:02.200
did this and that and talked it through.

216
00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:06.220
And I looked at him and he looked at me and we figured out who was going to get what.

217
00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:11.920
And Harold came over and says, I should just get rid of this, right?

218
00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:17.380
I'm like, no, Harold, this really, it's such an honor to be working with you.

219
00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:18.540
And yeah, yeah.

220
00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:19.380
Don't shit me, kid.

221
00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:20.680
I should get rid of this plan, right?

222
00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:22.360
This is what you did was way better.

223
00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:26.080
And I'm like, wow, that's a, it's a great roadmap, Harold.

224
00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:31.180
And, and then, you know, we did one more scene and I remember watching him looking at him and

225
00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:32.020
he got my attention.

226
00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:34.960
He just chucked his plans in Pam's trash bin.

227
00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:36.300
I remember exactly where we were.

228
00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:36.740
Yeah.

229
00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:44.580
Harold Ramis threw his shot plans into Pam's trash bin because he thought you nailed it.

230
00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:46.860
That, that's great, Randall.

231
00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:50.520
Well, that is, that would, for me, I'd be like, someone go get those out of the trash.

232
00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:51.440
I'm saving those.

233
00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:53.320
Yeah.

234
00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:54.120
Yeah.

235
00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:58.300
Well, I have to say, I was really curious what your answer was going to be to that question

236
00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:06.000
because my memory is that for the most part, it was you and Matt figuring out the shots

237
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:13.640
during our rehearsal and the directors were more focused on just actor performance, giving

238
00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:18.520
us notes, making sure we landed the jokes, making sure that the writers got their alternate

239
00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:19.400
jokes in.

240
00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:25.220
And really there was this huge trust that you guys knew how to shoot the show.

241
00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:30.900
The directors didn't, for the most part, like micromanage you in any way.

242
00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:37.240
I don't recall anybody ever telling me get to him on this line or miss this line.

243
00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:42.180
It was always, and you know, when I, when I went and it's, it's something I still do when

244
00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:47.300
I'm talking to cameras, I talk to a camera operator, like I'm talking to an actor.

245
00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:52.720
I will say, you think this, but you also suspect that she might do that.

246
00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:58.700
And that kind of gives the camera a little bit of a point of view, which for the office

247
00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:00.800
is paramount for other shows.

248
00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:04.600
I think is also interesting because you're trusting the intuition of the camera operator

249
00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:09.620
and you're giving them the basis to add some finesse to, to a scene, which I think

250
00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:11.100
is always really interesting.

251
00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:12.560
That's beautiful.

252
00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:13.780
I love that.

253
00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:19.700
Well, you know, Randall, one of the things you guys were also so great at was catching

254
00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:21.580
these moments that weren't scripted.

255
00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:26.440
I think that the way we filmed it added a level of authenticity as did the improvisational

256
00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:26.880
moments.

257
00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:31.740
And whether we'd use improvisational moments or not, it still put everybody in that space

258
00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:32.980
of this is happening.

259
00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:33.980
This is a real thing.

260
00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:35.220
There's an immediacy to this.

261
00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:36.440
It's not a rehearsed thing.

262
00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:39.740
It's something that is happening in front of us and around us.

263
00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:44.280
And for you all to be part of that was awesome.

264
00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:48.660
It was, it was amazing to see, you know, characters never drop character.

265
00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:53.960
They just kept Kevin, kept being Kevin and Angela, kept being Angela.

266
00:13:54.300 --> 00:14:02.940
And adding those moments in to me made it feel like a living thing and made it feel real

267
00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:09.120
and organic in a really meaningful and significant way that affected the overall outcome.

268
00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:10.620
That is so poetic.

269
00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:15.480
That is the best answer I have ever heard about improv on a set.

270
00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:20.960
Well, Randall, I know that we kept you in that office under the fluorescent light most

271
00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:26.880
of the time, but occasionally we did cram you into the backseat of a car or we put you

272
00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:30.640
on a boat, what was it like?

273
00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:32.860
What were those shoot days for you like?

274
00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:41.040
A shot note in a lot of awkward places, Einstein said, in the midst of difficulty lies opportunity.

275
00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:45.380
And the harder it got, the better it got.

276
00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:53.280
Like I just rewatched that scene in the car where we're driving to the lake and the whole

277
00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:57.300
time I was just reacting to it and, and, and I remember those, you know, in, in the midst

278
00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:02.260
of everything happening as difficult as it was with the water coming in and he cramped

279
00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:07.100
in the backseat when the GPS spoke, I panned to the GPS because it was a character in the

280
00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:07.360
scene.

281
00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:14.280
So those opportunities, I think add another element in a really, really interesting way.

282
00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:21.940
But like Michael's Sebring, which he was so proud of, it was a coop, it was a two door.

283
00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:27.900
So the backseat, although it's a four seater car or a five seater car, it wasn't, it wasn't

284
00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:30.640
a two seater plus a cameraman very comfortably.

285
00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:35.600
Cause I remember the camera came up higher than the back window because it was such a

286
00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:40.120
cool car, Michael, um, that the back window had such a slope to it that my camera would

287
00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:42.240
always hit the back of the, uh, of the window.

288
00:15:42.740 --> 00:15:47.300
And there was a hump there where the transmission went through and there's really kind of no place

289
00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:48.300
for your legs to go.

290
00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:53.720
So you just kind of tie yourself in a knot and, um, hopefully find some opportunity by

291
00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:54.360
that discomfort.

292
00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:57.280
You know, I get, I get car sick.

293
00:15:57.480 --> 00:15:58.740
I get really car sick.

294
00:15:58.880 --> 00:15:59.180
What?

295
00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:00.040
Yeah.

296
00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:02.180
Every time I got out of a car.

297
00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:04.020
How in the world do you do your job?

298
00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:04.980
Yeah.

299
00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:05.760
Oh my God.

300
00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:07.160
You get really car sick?

301
00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:07.760
Go on.

302
00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:09.300
Randall, I had no idea.

303
00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:14.280
Cause when we, so many times when we filmed in cars, we just looped, we did a circle and

304
00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:16.760
I would get nauseous just doing the circle.

305
00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:17.940
Yeah.

306
00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:20.020
Let alone only with one eye.

307
00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:21.320
Yeah.

308
00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:22.620
No, I get really car sick.

309
00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:27.440
And I remember every time Chris, Chris and Ed were helping me get, get the heavy camera

310
00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:29.100
out and helping me come out.

311
00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:30.080
They're like, how are you doing?

312
00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:30.520
You all right?

313
00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:31.020
You okay?

314
00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:31.700
Yeah.

315
00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:32.680
Real good.

316
00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:36.360
And I would all, it was, you know, it lasted a couple hours, but.

317
00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:40.660
We had, we had so many, we had so many driving scenes, Randall.

318
00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:41.920
I never knew this.

319
00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:42.840
Yeah.

320
00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:43.920
Oh my gosh.

321
00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:44.280
Yeah.

322
00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:46.120
I was getting a little green at times back there.

323
00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:46.940
I was just kind of sweating.

324
00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:55.420
Well, thank you because it really did produce some amazing, amazing things.

325
00:16:56.180 --> 00:16:57.120
That was fun.

326
00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:04.000
I know we're sort of talking about, um, like difficult camera positions and stuff, but Randall,

327
00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:09.820
you have to share with everybody, you introduced us to the Pootie Scootie.

328
00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:11.400
The Pootie Scootie.

329
00:17:11.540 --> 00:17:13.260
But did you invent that?

330
00:17:13.420 --> 00:17:15.080
Was that something Dale made for you?

331
00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:16.620
Dale made it for us.

332
00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:22.720
I, I, we were like, I would love to be able to scoot around the office at actor level and

333
00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:25.620
move, you know, not like on a dolly.

334
00:17:25.620 --> 00:17:31.020
And we just wanted to be quick and nimble and also so that I can react to it as opposed

335
00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:35.120
to communicating to a grip that I need to move back a foot because I'm going to pan to

336
00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:35.500
the left.

337
00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:36.900
We never would have gotten there.

338
00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:40.140
So Dale built those and it was great.

339
00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:42.600
That thing worked really, really well.

340
00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:43.340
It was so fun.

341
00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:45.020
It was like a rolling box.

342
00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:47.920
And then they eventually got you a little cushion on top of it.

343
00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:48.880
Right?

344
00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:49.170
Cushion.

345
00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:49.900
Those were the days.

346
00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:50.280
Yeah.

347
00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:50.840
Those were the days.

348
00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:52.740
And you could spin around.

349
00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:56.540
It was like, kind of like a stool, but a little better than a stool for you because I think

350
00:17:56.540 --> 00:17:57.660
it was like square.

351
00:17:58.880 --> 00:17:59.060
Yeah.

352
00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:03.420
Did you take the Pootie Scootie with you when you left the office?

353
00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:04.040
I didn't.

354
00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:04.720
I didn't.

355
00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:05.480
You didn't?

356
00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:06.640
No.

357
00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:07.880
No.

358
00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:11.840
And since, and since then I've had other Pootie Scooties built for me and they just

359
00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:12.800
weren't the same.

360
00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:14.600
Yeah.

361
00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:15.720
Yeah.

362
00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:22.720
Well, I feel like something I want to tell our audience is how incredibly rare it is

363
00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:25.660
for a cinematographer to also operate the camera.

364
00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:31.240
And it sounds like, Randall, you fought so hard to always keep that camera in your hands.

365
00:18:31.340 --> 00:18:32.580
You did not want to put it on sticks.

366
00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:34.100
You did not want to put it on a dolly.

367
00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:35.720
You were the operator.

368
00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:43.800
And in other projects I've done, it really is usually a cinematographer directing a camera

369
00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:44.460
operator.

370
00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:48.660
And they don't put their hands on the camera so much.

371
00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:50.340
It was like this really cool thing.

372
00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:51.000
Yeah.

373
00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:56.300
I think there's always an element of disconnect because on traditional shows, there's like

374
00:18:56.300 --> 00:19:01.520
eight to 10 people just, you know, who are looking after two cameras.

375
00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:04.960
And on our show, our camera department was four.

376
00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:13.540
It was me and Matt and Chris and Ed and Matt and I pulled our own focus and we pulled our

377
00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:14.180
own iris.

378
00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:18.320
I would set the look for the cameras, you know, I would program the cameras, what we

379
00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:20.500
wanted them to look like and how we wanted them to match.

380
00:19:21.020 --> 00:19:23.360
And then the rest of the stuff was just worked out in poSt.

381
00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:28.800
But Matt and I thought it was really important to pull our own focus and pull our own iris.

382
00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:37.460
I also think there's, again, a level of intimacy when the operator is part of the scene and is

383
00:19:37.460 --> 00:19:38.960
also listening to the scene.

384
00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:42.980
And that's something that doesn't happen on a lot of shows is quite often a lot of shows,

385
00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:44.660
the DP doesn't listen to the words.

386
00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:47.420
And I wonder, how do you know what to do?

387
00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:48.160
Yeah.

388
00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:54.400
Because coming up in documentaries, I didn't know what to shoot until my sound man put the

389
00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:56.840
boom up and said, this is a relevant conversation.

390
00:19:56.840 --> 00:19:58.300
You should be shooting this, idiot.

391
00:19:58.480 --> 00:19:59.140
Don't shoot that.

392
00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:03.980
So I still, hey, when I operate, I wear an earpiece.

393
00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:07.960
So I'm hearing what's going on so that I can finesse what I'm doing.

394
00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:11.300
Now, Randall, sometimes on our set, things got a little physical.

395
00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:13.360
Who ran into you?

396
00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:15.120
Who did you collide with the most?

397
00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:19.140
You know, I think I sent myself to the floor in the fire one.

398
00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:22.780
I remember that I was backing up through the kitchen and I fell.

399
00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:26.600
And I think I hit the ground, but I'm not real sure.

400
00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:32.140
But I'm a 200-pound human being and the camera is 38 pounds.

401
00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:36.060
And I remember Chris Workman, who's a very strong person,

402
00:20:36.060 --> 00:20:40.300
I think he picked me up with one hand and I just kept rolling.

403
00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:42.860
He just picked me up with one hand.

404
00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:43.980
I'm like, oh, did I fall?

405
00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:46.840
And I just kept going with the scene because I'm like, I think I fell.

406
00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:50.820
And Randall, that's the footage that stayed in.

407
00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:51.700
Yes.

408
00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:55.140
We used the take of you falling and getting back up.

409
00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:56.100
It was incredible.

410
00:20:56.460 --> 00:21:00.540
I remember being there and seeing it out of the corner of my eye and thinking,

411
00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:02.660
should we, are we still going?

412
00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:04.460
Because Randall is on the floor.

413
00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:05.860
Or, oh, no, Randall's up.

414
00:21:06.040 --> 00:21:06.480
Randall's up.

415
00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:06.800
All right.

416
00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:07.700
We're going to continue.

417
00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:08.820
Yep.

418
00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.720
That was Chris Workman just lifting me up like a little rag doll.

419
00:21:11.860 --> 00:21:13.180
And I'm letting me go.

420
00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:14.220
I was just back on my feet.

421
00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:15.560
Anyway.

422
00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:21.020
One thing I remember is in Benihana, I fell on the pootie scootie.

423
00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:23.800
I remember the last thing I saw was Steve just going, no.

424
00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:28.480
And it was always that.

425
00:21:28.600 --> 00:21:29.460
It was always Steve like, no.

426
00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:30.700
What about you?

427
00:21:32.120 --> 00:21:33.720
I want to ask this question.

428
00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:41.200
In addition to saving your life in that scene, Chris Workman also used to bring you this drink

429
00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:42.200
on set.

430
00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:44.820
And Angela and I need to know more about it.

431
00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:47.480
It looked like it was like fruit and water.

432
00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:49.380
What was this drink?

433
00:21:49.540 --> 00:21:51.800
Was this your special camera operator fuel?

434
00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:57.540
You see, that's the difference between being out in the hot sun in Thailand and shooting people

435
00:21:57.540 --> 00:21:59.900
on a beach and being in the office.

436
00:22:00.280 --> 00:22:04.740
I would not get the same type of beverages out there.

437
00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:07.720
You know, that was one of the perks.

438
00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:08.420
One of the perks.

439
00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:11.000
I mean, season three, I would have them.

440
00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:12.300
I like bubbly water.

441
00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:13.580
Yes, I was going to say.

442
00:22:13.660 --> 00:22:14.800
I remember it was fizzy.

443
00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:15.660
Yeah.

444
00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:17.580
I sound like such a fancy man.

445
00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:18.960
I like bubbly water.

446
00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:27.080
And I would have Chris bruise the fruit and put it in my water for me.

447
00:22:27.220 --> 00:22:30.860
And so I would have real fruit flavored bubbly water.

448
00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:33.300
Now, if that's not living, that's not a sign of success.

449
00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:34.380
I don't know what is.

450
00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:35.500
I love it.

451
00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:39.380
Chris made me one one time because I was like, Chris, where do you keep bringing Randall?

452
00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:39.960
I want one.

453
00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:43.040
And then he just sort of slyly slid one on my desk.

454
00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:45.140
I was like, it was so refreshing.

455
00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:47.580
I stand by it, Randall.

456
00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:48.860
I think it's a great move.

457
00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:53.260
And, you know, you're not you're not too fancy on your pootie scootie with your bubbly water.

458
00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:55.500
Yeah.

459
00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:58.080
OK, Randall, we do ask our guests this.

